WHERE TO START
--------------
With an ISMS under ISO/IEC 27001:
  Start with the integration matrix D-23. It shows what you reuse, extend
  or create from scratch. Supplementary sheets replace new documents. Guide
  value: about 90 days to stage 1 readiness for a small scope.

Without an ISMS:
  Start with the gap analysis D-00 and the guide. You use document control,
  internal audit, management review and nonconformities as separate
  documents (D-26, D-20, D-21, D-22). Guide value: about 150 days to
  stage 1 readiness.

With a link to the EU (customers, a branch or use of AI output in the EU):
switch on the EU AI Act module.

If US customers ask about the NIST AI RMF, or you want a measurement method
for your AI systems: switch on the NIST AI RMF module (guide chapter 10,
about 3 to 5 person-days extra). It is voluntary; NIST does not certify.


ROADMAP IN 10 STEPS
-------------------
 1. Mandate and starting point (D-00)
 2. Context, roles, scope, processes under 4.4 (D-01, D-02, D-27)
 3. Policy, roles, reporting (D-03, D-04)
 4. AI inventory (D-10)
 5. Role first, then classification (D-24, E-01, E-02)
 6. Risks and impact assessments (D-06 to D-09)
 7. Controls, statement of applicability, approval (D-07, D-11)
 8. Implement (D-12 to D-17, D-26)
 9. Measure, internal audit, management review, corrections (D-05, D-20 to D-22);
    if the audit precedes the first review, plan a supplementary audit before stage 1
10. Certification (D-25)


GOOD TO KNOW
------------
- Word and Excel: the templates are DOCX and XLSX files. Open them with
  Microsoft Office or with the free LibreOffice. No macros.
- Windows: Office first opens files from the internet in Protected View.
  Click ‘Enable Editing’. For all files at once: before extracting,
  right-click the ZIP file, ‘Properties’, tick ‘Unblock’, ‘OK’.
- Offline: the start page, the AIMS Manager and the diagram viewer do not
  connect to the internet. Your project data stays on your computer.
- Standard: the kit contains no text from the standard. You can obtain
  ISO/IEC 42001 from SNV, DIN or ISO.
- Newer versions: {{kurzlink}}
  (The start page does not check this itself.)


